root = Quiz("Trial of the Doctrine", [q1, q2, q3, q4, q5, q6])

q1 = QuizQuestion(
  "q1",
  "According to the six principles, when should accounts, licenses, and API keys be ready for a newcomer?",
  [
    QuizChoice(
      "a",
      "In pre-boarding — before day one",
      true,
      "Correct. Principle 1 'Access First' demands credentials exist before the newcomer arrives; waiting-for-access is the number-one onboarding killer.",
    ),
    QuizChoice(
      "b",
      "On Monday morning, requested by the newcomer's manager",
      false,
      "Requesting access on day one makes the newcomer watch a provisioning queue — the exact anti-pattern Principle 1 forbids.",
    ),
    QuizChoice(
      "c",
      "After the first week, once they've read the handbook",
      false,
      "Reading without access means touching nothing real; the doctrine drives newcomers to a real task in week one, which requires day-zero access.",
    ),
  ],
  "Principle 1 has a strong opinion about the calendar.",
)

q2 = QuizQuestion(
  "q2",
  "What is the only week-one metric the doctrine says matters most?",
  [
    QuizChoice(
      "a",
      "Number of handbook pages read",
      false,
      "Pages read is a vanity metric; the doctrine measures a completed real task, not consumed documentation.",
    ),
    QuizChoice(
      "b",
      "Time-to-first-value: how fast the newcomer completes one meaningful task",
      true,
      "Correct. Principle 2 declares time-to-first-value the only week-one metric that matters — first record, first safe change, or first successful API call.",
    ),
    QuizChoice(
      "c",
      "How many features the tour covered",
      false,
      "Feature-tour coverage is what Principle 3 rejects; competence comes from one deep golden-path task, not breadth.",
    ),
  ],
  "Principle 2 names it explicitly.",
)

q3 = QuizQuestion(
  "q3",
  "A developer signs up for your API and spends an afternoon fighting setup before one successful call. Which audience's doctrine did you break, and what is the metric called?",
  [
    QuizChoice(
      "a",
      "Web App end users — their metric is the first successful business task",
      false,
      "End users measure first business task inside the product; fighting an API afternoon is the developer archetype, whose metric is time-to-first-successful-API-call.",
    ),
    QuizChoice(
      "b",
      "Developers (DX) — their first value is time-to-first-successful-API-call on a clean machine",
      true,
      "Correct. For developers the onboarding IS the product trial; the taught metric is time-to-first-successful-API-call, and setup friction makes integrators quietly leave.",
    ),
    QuizChoice(
      "c",
      "Internal staff & operators — their metric is the first supervised deploy",
      false,
      "Operators graduate through graded real tasks ending in a supervised deploy; a self-serve API signup is the developer archetype's journey.",
    ),
  ],
  "Each archetype has its own flavor of first value.",
)

q4 = QuizQuestion(
  "q4",
  "Where does the doctrine say to reveal advanced features and edge-case warnings to a newcomer?",
  [
    QuizChoice(
      "a",
      "In the kickoff presentation, so nobody is surprised later",
      false,
      "Front-loading everything is the information firehose Principle 4 warns against — it creates quit-points, not competence.",
    ),
    QuizChoice(
      "b",
      "At the exact moment the newcomer reaches that step",
      true,
      "Correct. Progressive disclosure teaches the next step when it is needed, never three screens ahead.",
    ),
    QuizChoice(
      "c",
      "In an appendix of the handbook they can read anytime",
      false,
      "An appendix nobody opens is where knowledge goes to hide; the doctrine reveals complexity at the point of need.",
    ),
  ],
  "Principle 4 is about timing, not content volume.",
)

q5 = QuizQuestion(
  "q5",
  "A fully self-serve in-app tour ships for your Web App. According to the Human Anchor principle, what must still exist?",
  [
    QuizChoice(
      "a",
      "A longer tour that answers every possible question",
      false,
      "No tour answers everything; the doctrine's answer is not more software but a named human for the questions the tour cannot answer.",
    ),
    QuizChoice(
      "b",
      "A named human, buddy, or office hours where questions land",
      true,
      "Correct. Even self-serve paths need office hours or a buddy — unanswered questions at hour three are where motivation dies.",
    ),
    QuizChoice(
      "c",
      "A FAQ page link in the tour's final step",
      false,
      "A FAQ link is a dead end, not an anchor; the principle demands a real person or live office hours the question can reach.",
    ),
  ],
  "Principle 5 names the failure hour.",
)

q6 = QuizQuestion(
  "q6",
  "Your onboarding program has a documented dip: half of newcomers stall at the same screen. What does Principle 6 tell you to do?",
  [
    QuizChoice(
      "a",
      "Instrument the path, then fix the biggest measured quit-point before adding new content",
      true,
      "Correct. Measure the friction (login success, completion rate, ticket storms) and fix the biggest quit-point before writing another page.",
    ),
    QuizChoice(
      "b",
      "Write an extra handbook chapter explaining that screen",
      false,
      "Adding content before measuring is the doctrine's favorite anti-pattern — more pages, same quit-point.",
    ),
    QuizChoice(
      "c",
      "Send everyone a reminder email to read the wiki",
      false,
      "Reminder emails treat the symptom; Principle 6 sends you to the instrumentation to find and remove the cause.",
    ),
  ],
  "Measure first, then fix — in a specific order.",
)
